<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext, for exactly one purpose: reaching a sync host on the local network.

  The app's own origin is https://localhost, and Android has blocked cleartext
  HTTP by default since API 28 (this app targets 36). Without this, a sync to
  http://192.168.x.x is refused by the OS before it leaves the phone.

  Why it is scoped the way it is, and why it is not scoped more tightly:

  Android's network security config addresses hosts by name or literal address.
  It has no way to express "only RFC1918 ranges", so a home server on a dynamic
  address cannot be permitted without permitting cleartext generally. That is
  the whole of the trade being made here:

    - HTTPS traffic is untouched. The only outbound request the app makes over
      TLS is the release check against GitHub.
    - No data is sent anywhere by this permission. The app has no server, no
      account and no analytics; enabling cleartext opens a protocol, it does not
      open an endpoint.
    - It does mean that if the WebView were ever compromised by something the
      app loads, an attacker could exfiltrate over plain HTTP without a
      certificate to present. Accepted, because the alternative is a Sync
      setting that silently cannot work.

  Removing this file and the android:networkSecurityConfig attribute from the
  manifest reverts the app to cleartext-blocked; nothing else depends on it, and
  every other feature keeps working.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>